1
00:00:00,299 --> 00:00:04,340
The bar says five out of five, but we've only collected one coin.

2
00:00:04,395 --> 00:00:09,794
At the end, the game reports Failed, with one Coin. This time the
display is misleading.

3
00:00:09,819 --> 00:00:13,549
We'll use that deliberate fault to trace the data, fix the
binding,

4
00:00:13,574 --> 00:00:16,646
and check a separately imported copy of the whole woodland.

5
00:00:22,337 --> 00:00:27,517
We'll compare the HUD with the native log, inspect one binding,
and test a small repair.

6
00:00:27,601 --> 00:00:33,627
Then we'll export the corrected save, import a separate card, and
play the complete timed challenge there.

7
00:00:33,788 --> 00:00:38,113
Continue from the achievement lesson in a practice copy, keeping
the tested source available.

8
00:00:38,811 --> 00:00:43,194
Before changing anything, open the CoinProgress template's
Functions settings.

9
00:00:43,291 --> 00:00:47,354
Our correct Current Progress Value is Player Themself, Coins.

10
00:00:47,558 --> 00:00:54,157
Minimum is ProgressMin, and maximum is CoinGoal. Read those three
roles separately. This gives us a

11
00:00:54,182 --> 00:00:59,041
clear starting point for the fault, rather than trying to remember
what a working bar looked like.

12
00:00:59,875 --> 00:01:05,848
In this separate practice copy, change only Current Progress Value
to Player Themself, CoinGoal.

13
00:01:05,958 --> 00:01:12,133
Keep the minimum and maximum as they were. Use Save Changes,
Confirm Changes, and save the scene.

14
00:01:12,315 --> 00:01:16,250
We're intentionally making the display read the goal as though it
were the current count.

15
00:01:16,369 --> 00:01:18,096
The pickup graph hasn't been changed.

16
00:01:23,858 --> 00:01:26,875
Start a fresh private test and stop at the spawn point.

17
00:01:27,015 --> 00:01:32,443
The coins are still waiting, and Start hasn't been pressed, but
the bar already reads five out of five.

18
00:01:32,562 --> 00:01:36,733
That is our first useful observation. It tells us the full display
appeared

19
00:01:36,758 --> 00:01:40,854
before a successful pickup, so we shouldn't begin by adding
another counting node.

20
00:01:41,538 --> 00:01:46,808
Press F to start and walk through one coin. It disappears, but the
bar stays full.

21
00:01:46,939 --> 00:01:52,751
The unchanged bar doesn't tell us whether the counter changed. We
need another observation from the data path.

22
00:01:52,955 --> 00:01:58,454
Keep this attempt distinct from a retry, so the log and result we
read belong to the same test.

23
00:01:59,623 --> 00:02:05,542
In Miliastra Sandbox, open Window, then Log, and select the
intended server graph.

24
00:02:05,682 --> 00:02:10,790
In this attempt's log, Stage Start is followed by the start
message and Print, one.

25
00:02:10,844 --> 00:02:13,815
The pickup graph's print reads the stored Player Coins.

26
00:02:13,907 --> 00:02:18,055
That gives us a different answer from the full HUD, without adding
a second counter.

27
00:02:19,075 --> 00:02:22,306
This fault demonstration also reaches natural settlement.

28
00:02:22,446 --> 00:02:27,158
It reports Failed, with one Coin, while the incorrect HUD had
looked complete.

29
00:02:27,371 --> 00:02:32,637
The counter and the result agree on one. The display is the part
that disagrees. We now have a

30
00:02:32,662 --> 00:02:37,634
reason to inspect its source binding, rather than changing the
challenge goal to make the result fit.

31
00:02:38,428 --> 00:02:44,491
Return to the template. Coins is the amount collected. ProgressMin
is the empty end of the range.

32
00:02:44,716 --> 00:02:48,587
CoinGoal is the amount required for a full bar and the challenge
rule.

33
00:02:48,727 --> 00:02:52,841
Our mistake puts CoinGoal in both the current and maximum fields.

34
00:02:52,969 --> 00:02:57,572
With five compared against five, the bar looks full even when
Coins is zero.

35
00:02:58,409 --> 00:03:02,108
Change Current Progress Value back to Player Themself, Coins.

36
00:03:02,248 --> 00:03:05,232
Leave ProgressMin and CoinGoal in the range fields.

37
00:03:05,338 --> 00:03:12,224
Use Save Changes and Confirm Changes, then Quick Save the scene.
This repair changes the display's input.

38
00:03:12,249 --> 00:03:17,652
It keeps the tested pickup and settlement rules available, so we
can check whether the observations now agree.

39
00:03:23,329 --> 00:03:28,480
Run a fresh private test. Before Start, the display is empty at
zero out of five.

40
00:03:28,577 --> 00:03:34,997
Press F, collect one coin and stop. The bar now reads one out of
five with about twenty percent fill.

41
00:03:35,084 --> 00:03:38,208
Check this small case before repeating the entire route.

42
00:03:38,326 --> 00:03:41,854
It is enough to show that the repaired control follows a real
pickup again.

43
00:03:42,509 --> 00:03:48,857
Return to the editor and save the corrected practice copy. Give
the saved version a recognisable identity.

44
00:03:48,976 --> 00:03:54,749
We now want to check more than the currently open scene. An
exported file and a separately imported card

45
00:03:54,774 --> 00:04:00,919
will let us find out whether the whole project arrives with the
variables, layout, graphs and rules it needs.

46
00:04:01,680 --> 00:04:06,895
On Local Save, open the corrected card's menu and choose Export
Save File.

47
00:04:06,971 --> 00:04:10,586
Choose your own folder and retain the whole-save file format.

48
00:04:10,726 --> 00:04:16,226
The resource supplied with this lesson is Woodland, Episode Eight,
Restored. Its exact file

49
00:04:16,251 --> 00:04:20,944
is the one used in our import check. Keep the source card as well
as the exported file.

50
00:04:21,637 --> 00:04:25,836
Choose Import Save and select that exported whole-save file.

51
00:04:25,861 --> 00:04:30,629
Wait for the new card, then give it a distinct name. Our imported
copy is Woodland,

52
00:04:30,654 --> 00:04:35,848
Episode Eight, Restored Final. The folder chooser is outside the
captured views.

53
00:04:35,945 --> 00:04:41,234
Read the new card before opening it, so you can distinguish the
imported project from the practice source.

54
00:04:41,970 --> 00:04:47,526
Open the restored card. Check the stone trail, trees, shrubs and
five coins.

55
00:04:47,773 --> 00:04:53,382
A thumbnail is useful for finding a card, but it doesn't show that
its objects and rules arrived correctly.

56
00:04:53,469 --> 00:04:58,542
Look at several recognizable parts of the actual scene, including
the route the player will use.

57
00:04:59,375 --> 00:05:04,086
Inspect Default Player through Edit Player. Check the standard
variables Coins,

58
00:05:04,141 --> 00:05:09,612
ProgressMin and CoinGoal as Integers, with defaults zero, zero and
five.

59
00:05:09,688 --> 00:05:15,610
Check RoundStarted and RoundActive as Booleans, both No. These
definitions connect the pickup,

60
00:05:15,635 --> 00:05:20,471
interface and timed controller. A restored picture of the woodland
wouldn't replace them.

61
00:05:21,156 --> 00:05:26,336
In Timer Management, read Round Timer as Countdown, one hundred
and twenty seconds.

62
00:05:26,506 --> 00:05:30,824
In Default Layout, find CoinProgress, Timer and the Start button.

63
00:05:30,942 --> 00:05:35,346
Check that the active Custom Class selects that layout. The
timer's Player source

64
00:05:35,422 --> 00:05:39,374
and the button's instance reference still need to agree with the
imported controller.

65
00:05:45,162 --> 00:05:48,010
Start a fresh private test from this restored card.

66
00:05:48,106 --> 00:05:53,130
We see zero out of five and an inactive timer before Start. Press
F to begin.

67
00:05:53,355 --> 00:05:58,970
In this imported copy, a second F leaves the countdown decreasing.
This checks a real connection

68
00:05:59,002 --> 00:06:04,200
in the new card, rather than assuming every reference is correct
because import completed.

69
00:06:05,256 --> 00:06:10,876
Walk along the route and collect the coins. In this run, the HUD
reaches three out of five,

70
00:06:10,973 --> 00:06:15,641
then five out of five at the pavilion. The visible coins disappear
along the way.

71
00:06:15,725 --> 00:06:21,110
The shown movement stays at normal speed; standing and the longer
wait are shortened in the lesson.

72
00:06:21,880 --> 00:06:28,170
Let the full countdown reach zero naturally. The restored copy
reports Victory, with five Coins.

73
00:06:28,382 --> 00:06:34,300
That checks the complete path from its Start input through
pickups, live display and timed settlement.

74
00:06:34,355 --> 00:06:40,135
It is stronger evidence for this particular file than a successful
load message or a familiar-looking scene.

75
00:06:40,924 --> 00:06:45,250
Inspect Achievement Obtained as well. Our restored test shows
FreshStart,

76
00:06:45,275 --> 00:06:50,832
First Coin, ThreeCoins, All Five and Clear Run, together with the
Ultimate trophy.

77
00:06:51,036 --> 00:06:56,234
This is the private-test result for the imported file. It doesn't
establish public or permanent-account

78
00:06:56,259 --> 00:07:01,101
awards, but it does show the configured lesson goals completing in
this restored challenge.

79
00:07:01,968 --> 00:07:06,542
Keep the exported file beside a short test note: which imported
card you opened,

80
00:07:06,618 --> 00:07:09,059
what you checked, and the result you observed.

81
00:07:09,178 --> 00:07:15,102
The companion resources include the exact file used here and its
hash. If you change the project later,

82
00:07:15,126 --> 00:07:19,274
give that new version a distinct name and repeat the checks that
matter to the change.

83
00:07:20,123 --> 00:07:25,155
Try explaining the fault in one sentence: the bar read the goal
instead of the collected count.

84
00:07:25,262 --> 00:07:29,503
Then name the observations that supported the repair: a visible
pickup,

85
00:07:29,528 --> 00:07:34,039
the stored-count log, settlement, and the repaired zero-to-one
display.

86
00:07:34,222 --> 00:07:38,574
This is a useful way to narrow a problem before replacing parts
that already work.

87
00:07:39,305 --> 00:07:41,298
Keep your restored woodland available.

88
00:07:41,396 --> 00:07:47,329
It now gives us a recoverable example of the coin, HUD, timer,
result and achievement loop.

89
00:07:47,441 --> 00:07:52,451
In the final advanced lesson, we'll bring a small Blender-built
prop into a separate copy,

90
00:07:52,476 --> 00:07:57,590
using a supported primitive conversion workflow and checking its
actual appearance in the game.
